      "content": "Neighborhood stability is a great concept, but it's not as simple as just looking at zip codes or demographics. You're better off focusing on data points that reflect the actual quality of the neighborhood, like crime rates, poverty levels, and the percentage of residents with a high school diploma. Areas with high concentrations of single-parent households, low-income families, and minority populations tend to have higher default rates, so it's worth considering those factors when building your model.",
