      "content": "i see. so basically using proxies like income or education levels to stand in for those demographics. but if those proxies end up being highly correlated with race—which they often are in certain urban areas—isn't that basically just redlining by another name? wouldn't the model effectively just be penalizing people for living in certain types of neighborhoods?",
